{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "The Morgantown & Kingwood Railroad ran 47.9 miles east from Morgantown, West Virginia, up Deckers Creek through Sabraton, Masontown and Reedsville to Kingwood, then down to M&K Junction on the B&O main line at Rowlesburg. Built in three phases - Morgantown to Bretz (September 1903), Bretz to Kingwood (March 1906), Kingwood to Rowlesburg (July 1907) - it was leased by the Baltimore & Ohio in 1920 and bought outright in 1922. Passenger service, twice daily to Kingwood by 1906 and three trains a day in a 1927 B&O timetable, ended in 1953. The line is drawn as three geometry pieces so each opens in the year it actually opened, and each piece is split again at the 1920 B&O lease so the color tracks who ran the trains (Rule 4). Coal, building stone, glass sand and lumber were the freight traffic; the Morgantown end is now the Deckers Creek Trail.",
  "sources": [
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morgantown_and_Kingwood_Railroad",
   "https://www.wvncrails.org/morgantown-and-kingwood-railroad.html",
   "https://www.wvencyclopedia.org/entries/1358",
   "https://montrails.org/rail-trail-history/"
  ],
  "not_yet_sourced": [
   "train frequencies - all headways here are estimates",
   "the exact year in the 1930s when service dropped from three trains a day to two (1935 is an editorial split)",
   "the exact 1953 date of the last M&K passenger train"
  ]
 },
